# Seam definitions, providers, and a consumer.
#
# This is the part docs/design.md §3.2 calls the reason to build the harness at
# all: a seam is a `protocol`, and the protocol carries the *authority
# contract*. A provider is bounded by the interface it implements rather than
# by its own good manners, which is the thing a TypeScript harness cannot do.
#
# The three roles of a seam (design §1.1) are all here, deliberately — one role
# alone is not a seam:
#
#   Service Definition  HarnessFs           the protocol, and the contract
#   Service Provider    LocalFs / MemFs     two implementations
#   Consumer            head_tool           written against the protocol
#
# See demo.gene for the payoff: swapping the provider changes what the consumer
# does *and* what authority the whole product needs, without the consumer being
# touched.

(mod seams)

# --- Service Definition ----------------------------------------------------
#
# The `^capabilities` row is the authority envelope for every provider of this
# seam. An impl may restate it or declare less; it may not declare more.
#
# **A provider must state its row explicitly.** Omitting `^capabilities`
# entirely is rejected as *broadening* — an absent row means unchecked
# authority, which is wider than any declared one. So reading a provider tells
# you its authority without cross-referencing the protocol, which is the more
# useful arrangement even though it costs a line.

(protocol HarnessFs
  (message read_text [path : Str] : Str
    ^capabilities [(fs/ReadDir "/tmp")]))

# --- Service Provider: the real one ----------------------------------------

(type LocalFs ^props {^root Str})

(impl HarnessFs for LocalFs
  (message read_text [path : Str] : Str
    ^capabilities [(fs/ReadDir "/tmp")]
    # A real provider would read the file. What matters for the demo is the
    # declared row: this message cannot be called without the grant, so a
    # harness wired to this provider needs `--allow_read_dir /tmp` to run.
    $"local:${self/root}/${path}"))

# --- Service Provider: the test double -------------------------------------
#
# `^capabilities []` is narrower than the contract and therefore accepted. It
# is also honest: an in-memory provider needs no filesystem authority, and
# says so where anyone reading it can see.

(type MemFs ^props {^files Any})

(impl HarnessFs for MemFs
  (message read_text [path : Str] : Str
    ^capabilities []
    (?? (self/files ~ get path) $"mem:<no ${path}>")))

# --- The seam's own conformance gate ---------------------------------------
#
# The kernel's seam table is keyed by a plain string and holds `Any`, so the
# kernel cannot know that the value bound to "HarnessFs" implements HarnessFs.
# It does not have to: a protocol works as an annotation, so the Service
# Definition can supply the gate, which is its job anyway.
#
# Route a provider through this on the way into `provide` and a non-conforming
# value is refused at bind time instead of at first use.

(fn fs_provider [p : HarnessFs] : HarnessFs p)

# --- A second seam, varying in behaviour rather than authority -------------
#
# `HarnessFs` providers differ in what *authority* they need. This one does
# not: rendering text needs nothing from outside the process, so the contract
# is `^capabilities []` and every provider of it is equally harmless.
#
# The pair is deliberate. A seam is worth having when providers differ in
# behaviour *or* in authority, and a system with only the second kind would
# suggest capabilities are the only reason to draw a seam. They are not — they
# are the reason a seam can be trusted once drawn.

(protocol HarnessRender
  (message render [text : Str] : Str
    ^capabilities []))

(type TextRender)

(impl HarnessRender for TextRender
  (message render [text : Str] : Str
    ^capabilities []
    text))

(type HtmlRender)

(impl HarnessRender for HtmlRender
  (message render [text : Str] : Str
    ^capabilities []
    $"<p>$($html/escape text)</p>"))

(fn render_provider [r : HarnessRender] : HarnessRender r)

# --- A third seam: what handles a prompt -----------------------------------
#
# The agent seam. A real deployment binds a model-backed provider here; the one
# in this repo interprets commands, and the point of the seam is that the
# consumer — the terminal loop, an HTTP handler, a test — cannot tell.
#
# **This protocol deliberately declares no `^capabilities` row**, and that is
# not an oversight. The other two seams declare one because a provider of them
# does a specific, boundable thing: read a file, render text. A prompt handler
# dispatches — what it may reach is whatever the *deployment* allows, and a row
# here would be a ceiling on every provider forever. Since a declared row is
# transitive (a `[]` function cannot call a capability-declaring impl even when
# the process holds the grant), declaring one on a dispatcher would forbid it
# from delegating to the very seams it exists to delegate to.
#
# Authority still lands where it belongs: the narrow operations the handler
# performs *itself* declare their own rows at their own definitions, so
# `build` — which writes a plugin into the package — is bounded to one
# directory while `status` and `help` need nothing.

(protocol HarnessPrompt
  (message handle [h, prompt : Str] : Str))

(fn prompt_provider [p : HarnessPrompt] : HarnessPrompt p)

# --- Consumers -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Written against the seams. They name `HarnessFs` and `HarnessRender`, never
# `LocalFs`, `MemFs`, `TextRender` or `HtmlRender`, which is what makes one
# provider swap move every consumer at once.

(fn head_tool [fs, path : Str] : Str
  (fs ~ HarnessFs:read_text path))

(fn report_tool [fs, r, path : Str] : Str
  # Two seams, one consumer, and no knowledge of which providers are bound.
  # Under the `cli` profile this reads a real file and returns plain text;
  # under `web` it reads memory and returns escaped HTML. This function is
  # identical in both, and so is its source.
  (r ~ HarnessRender:render (head_tool fs path)))
